WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.940
- Legal Eagle is coming in loud and clear

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.890
thanks to Ting Mobile.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:05.360
Look, I am as surprised as anyone

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.220
that Florida isn't the swing
state in the 2020 election,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.600
nor is there any kind

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.440
of Floridian Bush v Gore style lawsuit

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.610
that seems like it's going
to change the outcome

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.750
of the election, which
is especially surprising

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:20.020
given that we have a literal
Florida man in the White House.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.980
There is no election chaos in Florida.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.570
Why did Florida get its act together,

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.710
while smaller states
took days and even weeks

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.543
to finalize their votes,

00:00:26.543 --> 00:00:28.720
and the vote is still currently ongoing?

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.110
Because Florida let counties
start counting early votes

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.890
and mailed ballots 22 days
before the Election day,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.500
and while this seems
like basic common sense,

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.345
not all states took this common sense,

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:41.820
dare I say, Floridian
approach to vote counting.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.750
So while the counting continues,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.210
President Trump and his
campaign and affiliated groups

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.585
have filed several lawsuits
challenging many aspects

00:00:48.585 --> 00:00:51.310
of the voting process
in the 2020 election.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.940
So today we are going to examine

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.720
how these election lawsuits work.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.700
Do any of the Trump campaign's lawsuits

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.920
stand a chance of getting
to the Supreme Court,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.940
and are any of these
lawsuits going to change

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.225
the outcome of the election?

00:01:03.225 --> 00:01:05.892
(rousing music)

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.760
Hey Legal Eagles, it's time to think

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.920
like the Trump campaign's lawyers.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.070
As we've talked about many
times on this channel,

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.500
you can sue anyone for anything
at any time for any reason

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.470
in America, but those claims
need to have a legal basis

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.190
and eventually you'll
need to provide evidence,

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.630
otherwise those lawsuits
will be thrown out.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.170
Now, maybe I am an
institutionalist big firm lawyer

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.750
but I think there is a silver lining

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.490
to all of these lawsuits,
whether they have merit or not.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.250
There are a lot of people who have doubts

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.540
about the 2020 election,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.910
whether those doubts
are well founded or not,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.140
so it's important for
those doubts to be tested.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.960
That's how we have confidence

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.340
in the whole election process in general,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.550
and the courts have
mechanisms for dealing with

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.060
both meritorious claims
and frivolous claims,

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.530
and if the claims are frivolous,

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.010
then they will be thrown out,

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.130
and if they have merit,

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.920
then the courts will fashion a remedy.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.020
And I think that if the
shoe was on the other foot,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.180
that it was clear that the Trump campaign

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.640
had won the election,
Democrats would want to know

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.880
if there was foul play involved or not,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.280
and that's what the
system is designed to do.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.230
But as it happens,

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.490
Joe Biden is the putative President-Elect,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.780
and it appears that Donald Trump

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.460
is going to be a one term President,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.020
which means that the
balance of the lawsuits

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.870
are going to come from the
Trump campaign in an attempt to,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.680
if not outright change the
outcome of the election,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.500
at least take a deeper
dive and, let's say,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.790
graciously make sure that
everything was on the up and up.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.000
Now before we dig into these
different state lawsuits,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.930
it's important to
recognize that every state

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:30.763
has a different law

00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:33.100
when it comes to how the
elections are going to be run.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.070
Some require that ballots
have to be received

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:37.170
before Election day.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.680
Some have a law that say as
long as the ballot is postmarked

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.460
by Election day, it will be accepted.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.590
So there's no overarching federal law,

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:45.970
every state has a different law,

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.090
and sometimes these laws
are sort of contradictory

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.730
so that it's a complete patchwork of laws

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:52.740
throughout the country.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.600
Now, not all of these election
lawsuits are created equal,

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.680
in fact, some have already been dismissed,

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.470
and these lawsuits so far have
fallen into two categories.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.250
One are claims of fraud,

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.900
and then two are claims of poll watchers

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.110
not being able to adequately observe

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.870
the poll process itself.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.640
The fraud claims have so far
generally been roundly rejected

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.730
and disproved, while the observation suits

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.760
have gained some traction.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.490
We'll start relatively chronologically,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.020
starting with some of the suits

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.010
that have already been bounced,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.020
and then we'll end with the
most consequential lawsuit

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.710
that may, in fact, end
up in the Supreme Court,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.930
those stemming out of Pennsylvania.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.110
And at the end of this video,

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.330
I will summarize what
effect these lawsuits

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:35.100
are likely to have on the
outcome of the 2020 Election.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.180
But we'll start with Georgia,
where there was a challenge

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.590
to the counting of
votes in Chatham County.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.370
Now you would think that if
you are trying to dispute

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.510
an election through lawsuit,

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.330
you would be trying to disallow
votes that had been counted.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:47.930
But currently there's only one lawsuit

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.670
that is directly challenging the counting

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.820
of identified ballots.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.130
On November 4th, the Trump
campaign filed a lawsuit

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.150
alleging that Chatham
County, Georgia was accepting

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.720
absentee ballots after the
state's statutory deadline.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.190
This would make those ballots invalid,

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.700
which means that they
wouldn't count, obviously.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.580
This lawsuit challenged
the way that Chatham County

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.540
was applying the Georgia law
concerning absentee ballots.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.900
Under that law, votes by
mail that are received

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.650
after 7:00 PM on Election
day will be kept unopened

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.868
and transferred to storage,

00:04:14.868 --> 00:04:17.430
where they will eventually be destroyed.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.120
The Trump campaign provided an affidavit

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.760
from someone claiming to be a poll watcher

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.660
who claimed that 53 absentee ballots

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:25.350
had been placed in a group
of ballots to be processed.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.470
The poll watcher claimed
that those 53 ballots

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:28.780
arrived after the deadline.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.210
However, the poll watcher
offered no actual proof

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.090
of this allegation,

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.780
and wasn't present when
the ballots were processed.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.560
Chatham County is home to Savannah,

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.540
an area where there are more
Democrats than Republicans,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.190
and after a one hour hearing,
the judge dismissed the case

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.220
with a one paragraph order,

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.250
explaining that the Trump
campaign didn't have any evidence

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.350
to support its factual claims.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.610
Now there will probably
be a recount in Georgia

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.210
and that process will include
examining the kinds of errors

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.277
that the Trump campaign alleged

00:04:53.277 --> 00:04:55.250
made these ballots defective.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.700
But as you can see, this
will entail a close reading

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.626
of the state election laws,

00:04:58.626 --> 00:05:01.710
and then a determination
about which ballots are valid

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.400
and when those ballots were received.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.270
This might change the final vote count

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.210
but there's probably
not enough impact here

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.310
to change the results in Georgia.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:11.460
Georgia is probably the only
state where the final vote

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.610
could be challenged due to
the problems with ballots.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:15.443
Keep in mind, however,

00:05:15.443 --> 00:05:17.900
that voters are already curing defects

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.040
in some of these ballots,

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.720
and so where the actual vote
tally is going to end up,

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.410
in terms of more votes for
Trump or fewer votes for Trump

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:28.290
is anyone's guess, and it's
possible that the Trump campaign

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.360
might file lawsuits
challenging the state's process

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.430
for curing ballots, but those
cases seem unlikely to end up

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.730
in the Supreme Court or have a number

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.190
that's appreciably going
to change the difference.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:39.880
So we move on to Michigan,

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.640
where there was a lawsuit
about poll observers.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.300
Now, Joe Biden is the
projected winner in Michigan

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.290
and on November 4th, the Trump campaign

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.600
and Republican self appointed
poll challenger Eric Ostergren

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:53.270
sued Michigan Secretary
of State Jocelyn Benson

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:54.890
in the Michigan court of claims.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.820
The lawsuit claims that
Ostergren was excluded

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.060
from watching the absentee
ballot counting board

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.030
and that the state had violated
the Michigan constitution

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:04.034
and Michigan election
law by processing ballots

00:06:04.034 --> 00:06:07.010
without allowing poll
watchers to view video footage

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.850
of voters placing ballots in drop boxes.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.490
Now the Trump campaign asked the court

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.880
to one, stop the counting
of absentee ballots

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.310
until an election inspector
from each party was present

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.180
at each absentee counting board,

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.830
and until video was made
available to challengers

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.410
of each ballot box, and
two, immediately set aside

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:25.517
all ballots that were not being, quote,

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:28.110
"inspected and monitored as described".

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.120
Ostergren's claim was
dismissed by the court

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.770
because he claimed he was removed

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.440
from an absent voter counting board,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.550
but didn't identify which board

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.820
or which official removed
him from the board.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:39.790
This means that he didn't
sue the proper defendant

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.870
and his claim failed at step one,

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:44.330
which is stating a claim for legal relief.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.480
But the lawsuit did include
affidavits from poll watchers

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.380
alleging that they had heard
people talking about fraud.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.945
For example, poll watcher Jessica Connarn

00:06:50.945 --> 00:06:53.170
said that another poll worker told her

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:54.844
that she had heard other poll workers

00:06:54.844 --> 00:06:57.390
say that they could
change the date on ballots

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.700
when they were received and
entering them into the computer.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.010
And when you're asking
for extraordinary relief,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.530
like what a lot of these
election lawsuits are asking for,

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.247
you need to have generally
extremely strong evidence,

00:07:07.247 --> 00:07:09.560
and here, that evidence just wasn't there.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.480
There was no verification of
the facts through evidence

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.300
and a lot of the evidence
was not admissible.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.490
The case was dismissed
by Michigan State Court

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.080
on November 6th, and admittedly,

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.539
I chuckled when some of my
Twitter followers pointed out

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:23.860
that this is one of the few
cases where hearsay evidence

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.520
was not admitted, and rightfully so.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.573
We have examples of
double and triple hearsay

00:07:27.573 --> 00:07:29.920
with no exceptions to the hearsay rule.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.380
It made sense that a lot of
this evidence was not admitted.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:34.647
The judge found that, quote,

00:07:34.647 --> 00:07:36.867
"The assertion that the
affiant was informed

00:07:36.867 --> 00:07:38.791
"by an unknown individual what, quote,

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:41.857
"'other hired poll workers
at her table' had been told

00:07:41.857 --> 00:07:43.747
"is inadmissible hearsay within hearsay

00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:45.717
"and plaintiffs have
provided no hearsay exception

00:07:45.717 --> 00:07:47.137
"for either level of hearsay

00:07:47.137 --> 00:07:49.077
"that would warrant
consideration of the evidence.

00:07:49.077 --> 00:07:52.830
"See MRE 801", which
is the Michigan version

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.500
of the rules of evidence.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.100
Now, a lot of these
cases sought TROs or PIs,

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.440
temporary restraining orders
or preliminary injunctions,

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.068
which is basically an
early order from the court

00:08:02.068 --> 00:08:04.180
seeking to maintain the status quo

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.960
while the rest of the
case can be litigated.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.370
Now, we talk a lot on this channel

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.120
about how you often don't
need to provide evidence

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.420
when you file a lawsuit,
but to get a TRO or a PI,

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:16.740
you do have to provide
usually very strong evidence.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.670
You'll often hear about
a verified complaint.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:20.460
Well, a verified complaint
just means a complaint

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.520
with actual evidence attached to it.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.020
But at this TRO or PI stage,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.290
a court will often test that evidence.

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.810
So a lot of times, when
you're seeing the courts

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.130
weighing evidence in these cases,

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.730
it's because the cases are
asking for a TRO or a PI,

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.760
which takes us to the
next Michigan lawsuit,

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.960
which sought a de-certification
of the election results.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.870
The Republican party wanted an injunction

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:44.090
barring Wayne County from
certifying any results

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.510
until representatives of both
parties examined all ballots.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:50.190
They also requested that all
ballot containers be unsealed

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:51.440
and prepared for inspection,

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.750
and that they'd be permitted
to take depositions

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.090
of local officials.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:57.710
A judge denied their
application for an injunction.

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:01.130
Now the Michigan law appears
to allow for local city clerks

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.029
to appoint Democratic
and Republican inspectors

00:09:03.029 --> 00:09:06.690
who can observe the counting
process, that's not in dispute.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:07.557
The judge found, quote,

00:09:07.557 --> 00:09:10.107
"Both Republican and Democratic
inspectors were present

00:09:10.107 --> 00:09:12.460
"throughout the absent voter
counting board location."

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:13.900
Court said that the plaintiffs had failed

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.040
to even state a cause of action,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.010
simply asserting widespread
fraud was not sufficient.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.127
The court stated, "The
only substantive allegation

00:09:20.127 --> 00:09:22.797
"appears in paragraph 15 of
the First Amended Complaint,

00:09:22.797 --> 00:09:25.007
"where Plaintiffs' allege
'on information and belief'

00:09:25.007 --> 00:09:27.407
"that hundreds or thousands
of ballots have been impacted

00:09:27.407 --> 00:09:28.957
"by this improper practice.

00:09:28.957 --> 00:09:31.647
"Plaintiff's Supplemental
Motion fails to present

00:09:31.647 --> 00:09:33.047
"any further specifics.

00:09:33.047 --> 00:09:34.267
"In short, the motion is based

00:09:34.267 --> 00:09:37.070
"upon speculation and conjecture."

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:39.110
And as anyone who watches
the Simpsons knows,

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.440
- We've got plenty of
hearsay and conjecture,

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.590
those are kinds of evidence.

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:45.860
- They're just not allowed in court.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:48.030
So this lawsuit seemed to
follow the general pattern

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.980
established in the Michigan lawsuits

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.020
where the Trump campaign
was making serious claims

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.101
about fraud without any
particularized evidence,

00:09:54.101 --> 00:09:56.110
claiming that Republicans had been denied

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.960
the opportunity to observe the count,

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.550
even though it seemed that
the contradictory evidence

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.610
showed that, in fact, they were present

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.570
and that there were impartial observers.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.500
And the thing about these
poll observer lawsuits

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.320
is that even if a judge sided
with the claims of the GOP

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:11.980
that certain poll watchers
were not treated fairly

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.300
or that they were excluded,
which seems relatively suspect,

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.210
but even taking it at face
value, if that were true,

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.660
it's unlikely that any judge
would invalidate the election,

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.770
because just because
observers weren't there

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:24.200
or were too far away,

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.580
doesn't mean that anything
untoward was happening.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.775
So without evidence of actual
widespread ballot fraud,

00:10:29.775 --> 00:10:32.317
it's unlikely a court
is going to invalidate

00:10:32.317 --> 00:10:35.980
a single ballot, let
alone the entire election.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:38.270
And interestingly, the
appeal of this Michigan case

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.640
was already rejected for
failing to file basic documents

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:42.540
with the appeal.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.780
Things like attaching the
judgment that they're appealing

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:46.820
and the transcript of the
case and the evidence.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.730
A big firm associate would be fired

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.400
for making these kind of mistakes,

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.970
so that might be an indication
of the kind of quality

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:55.370
of legal representation that's responsible

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.920
for the Michigan cases.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:58.880
So that takes us to the
great state of Nevada.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:01.370
Now in Nevada, Republicans
filed a single federal lawsuit

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:03.280
lumping several claims together.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.755
The alleged irregularities included

00:11:04.755 --> 00:11:07.870
what the complaint
described as lax procedures

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:09.440
for authenticating mailed ballots

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.210
and claiming that more
than 3000 ineligible voters

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:13.750
had cast a ballot.

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:16.230
One plaintiff is Jill
Stokke, a registered voter

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.470
who is 79 years old and legally blind.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:19.950
Stokke claimed that on Election day,

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.100
she went to vote in person

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.210
but was told that her ballot
had already been received

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:24.100
by election officials

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.090
and her signature was already verified.

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.250
Election officials met with her

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:28.990
and said that the signature on her ballot

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:30.580
matched the signature
that they had on file,

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:33.020
and since Stokke disputed
that it was her ballot,

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:34.660
the County still gave Stokke the option

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.630
to challenge her verified mail-in ballot

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.710
and cast a provisional ballot instead,

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:41.507
but she refused to cast
a provisional ballot.

00:11:41.507 --> 00:11:42.810
The Nevada Secretary of State

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:45.010
investigated her claim on November 3rd.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.150
Stokke told them that a
person who had been evicted

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:49.430
from her home had
probably stolen her ballot

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:52.120
and turned it in, but she
didn't want to name that person

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:53.950
and subject them to criminal charges.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.330
A Nevada election official
tried to persuade Stokke

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:58.620
to name the person,
make a formal statement

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:01.400
and vote by provisional
ballot, but she refused.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.220
The Nevada official also told Stokke

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.429
that if she cast another ballot,

00:12:04.429 --> 00:12:06.950
she would have double
voted, which is a crime.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.620
Stokke then contacted the
Trump campaign and the media.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.230
Now it seems that Stokke's claims

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.510
failed at the evidentiary
stage of the process.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.530
Her lawsuit claims that she
was turned away from the polls

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.738
on November 3rd, but documents
released by the states

00:12:17.738 --> 00:12:19.840
show that she talked with local officials

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:22.470
about the stolen ballot on October 28th,

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:23.877
during the early voting process.

00:12:23.877 --> 00:12:25.720
The Stokke allegations
are particularly egregious

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.210
because her lawsuit
declaration do not even say

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.090
that the ballot was stolen,

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:30.840
but that's what the Trump
campaign tweeted out,

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.330
which made her sort of internet star.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:35.833
A federal judge held a two hour
hearing to assess her claims

00:12:35.833 --> 00:12:37.740
and found them to be lacking.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.270
He also warned Stokke's lawyer

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:41.500
that she might have committed perjury.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:43.480
The judge also noted that the
remedy that Stokke sought,

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:45.830
an immediate halt to
counting all of the votes,

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.840
bore no relationship to her legal claims.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:49.920
The lawsuit also alleges

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.460
that Clark County's automated
signature verification machine

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.730
operates below manufacturer specifications

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:57.170
and that use of the machine
violates requirements

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:58.280
in state election law.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.850
The Trump campaign already
raised this same claim

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:02.165
in a separate lawsuit, which was dismissed

00:13:02.165 --> 00:13:03.890
a week before the election.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:05.540
In this complaint, the plaintiffs claim

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:07.330
that the use of the
machine is impermissible

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:09.050
under Nevada election code

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:10.680
and therefore violates
the Elections Clause

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:13.029
of the US Constitution,
and that Clark County's use

00:13:13.029 --> 00:13:16.130
of the machine constitution
equal protection violation,

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.610
but there doesn't appear to
very much merit in these claims.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:21.210
Nevada law authorizes
counties to adopt procedures

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.850
that include the processing
and counting of mailed ballots

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.350
by electronic means.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.880
The Clark County register opted
to use the sorting machine

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:29.070
to increase the efficiency

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.180
with which the mail
ballots were processed,

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:33.190
and the machine makes the first pass

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:34.170
on the sorting of ballots

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:35.680
to ensure the signature on the ballot

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:37.650
matches the signature of the registration,

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.350
but if the machine flags
a ballot for a mismatch,

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.200
then a person then reviews the ballot.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:44.810
The plaintiffs allege
that the process is faulty

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.040
because the machine does the initial pass

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:47.960
rather than the person.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:49.380
However, Clark County's process

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.550
appears to comply with Nevada law.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:54.450
Before a ballot can be
rejected, at least two employees

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.030
in the office of the clerk must agree

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.430
that there is a reasonable
question of fact

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:00.550
as to whether the signature
used for the mailed ballot

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:02.370
matches the signature of the voter.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.630
Here, the machine does not
make the final decision

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.150
about whether a ballot
will be rejected or not.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:09.167
A federal judge dismissed
the claim, stating that,

00:14:09.167 --> 00:14:11.257
"Nevada law allows for the use of machines

00:14:11.257 --> 00:14:13.537
"in tabulating votes, and
doesn't specifically forbid

00:14:13.537 --> 00:14:15.290
"signature comparison devices."

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:18.070
The lawsuit also includes
a poll observer component.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:20.380
Here, a man named Chris Prudhome,

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:22.350
who the lawsuit describes
as a credentialed member

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:24.720
of the media, also sued
Clark County officials.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.280
However, it appears that Prudhome

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:29.110
is actually a senior advisor
to the State Republican Party,

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.810
and Prudhome says that
officials didn't let him observe

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:35.140
ballot counting when
he arrived at 12:45 AM.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:36.490
The County officials simply said

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.480
that the counting was
over for the evening.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.440
Prudhome's declaration, which
is the evidence submitted

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.838
to the court in support of the claim,

00:14:41.838 --> 00:14:44.980
said that he was then improperly
told to leave the area.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:46.870
While Prudhome alleges that he was blocked

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:48.320
from observing the poll counting,

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:51.010
Clark County attorney
Mary-Anne Miller testified

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:52.920
that he was actually
removed for trying to record

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:55.420
the voting counting process,
which is not allowed.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.210
Prudhome also says that
he was forced to sit

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:58.880
too far away from the action.

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.090
He also alleges that it was
illegal for poll workers

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:03.660
to be behind the glass
partition, which makes it hard

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:06.290
for observers to hear what
the workers are saying.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.080
However, at the hearing,
Judge Gordon threw cold water

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:10.242
on Prudhome's complaints.

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:12.160
For mail ballots, Nevada law says

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:14.837
that once the counting board
begins counting ballots, quote,

00:15:14.837 --> 00:15:16.880
"The counting procedure must be public."

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:18.407
In an oral argument, the judge said,

00:15:18.407 --> 00:15:20.297
"You're asking me to
impose some new standard

00:15:20.297 --> 00:15:21.967
"or strictures or guidelines.

00:15:21.967 --> 00:15:23.167
"Do we have to provide microphones?

00:15:23.167 --> 00:15:25.740
"At what point does it
get into the ridiculous?"

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:27.540
Of course, Nevada law does allow people

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.760
to have meaningful public
access to vote counting.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.290
Here, the judge essentially ruled

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.310
that the counting procedure was public

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.140
and that Prudhome observed it.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:36.930
And in Nevada, a lot of
these claims of fraud

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:38.470
are already evaporating.

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:40.535
People were claiming that there were votes

00:15:40.535 --> 00:15:41.940
associated with dead people.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.510
It appears that most of
these are probably related

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:47.750
to service members who
since moved after voting,

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.950
which comports with the video
I did on election fraud,

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.150
it's usually just clerical errors.

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:55.060
So that takes us to Pennsylvania,
which is likely home

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:57.170
to the most consequential
election lawsuits

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:58.320
for several reasons.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:15:59.970
First, given how close the election is

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:03.420
and how many electoral votes
are available in Pennsylvania.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:05.583
Pennsylvania is sort of the equivalent

00:16:05.583 --> 00:16:09.310
of 2000 Florida in this
particular election.

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:11.270
And on top of that,
the lawsuits in Florida

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:14.840
have already gotten the attention
of the US Supreme Court.

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:16.424
So let's look at the different facets

00:16:16.424 --> 00:16:19.180
of these election lawsuits.

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:21.440
The first concerns late arriving ballots.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:23.150
The most high profile case in Pennsylvania

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:25.920
involves ballots arriving
after the state's deadline

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.480
for receipt for mail ballots.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:28.990
This is also the only case

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.490
currently before the Supreme Court.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.760
Well, it's sort of in
front of the Supreme Court,

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:33.730
which we'll get to.

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:36.110
The case began when the
Pennsylvania Democratic Party

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.350
filed a lawsuit that challenged

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.130
parts of the state's
absentee ballot system

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:41.230
in light of the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:43.640
This is long before the actual election.

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.040
Relying on a provision in
the state's constitution,

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:48.120
the Pennsylvania Supreme
Court extended the deadline

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.990
for mailed in ballots until November 6th.

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:53.680
As it stands now, ballots
that are clearly postmarked

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.470
after the election would not be counted

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:57.650
under the Pennsylvania court's ruling.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:00.120
Ballots postmarked on
or before Election day

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:02.770
and ballots with no postmark
or an unclear postmark

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:06.300
would be counted if received
by the new deadline.

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:08.903
However, Republicans objected
to the extending of a deadline

00:17:08.903 --> 00:17:12.340
from November 3rd to
November 6th, so they filed

00:17:12.340 --> 00:17:14.325
a separate case in federal courts.

00:17:14.325 --> 00:17:17.530
On September 28th, Republican legislators

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:19.360
and the Pennsylvania Republican Party

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:20.290
asked the Supreme Court

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:23.070
to put the Pennsylvania
Court's ruling on hold.

00:17:23.070 --> 00:17:25.250
The US Supreme Court
declined to hear the case

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:26.083
before the election.

00:17:26.083 --> 00:17:29.010
However, four Justices explicitly
left open the possibility

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.530
of reconsidering the case after the vote.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.160
But since the Supreme
Court left the door open,

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:36.230
Pennsylvania officials
decided to play it safe

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:38.480
and segregate the ballots
that were postmarked

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.070
on or before the Election day,

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:42.450
which arrived up to November 6th.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:45.400
These are postmarked before election,

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.500
but arrived after Election day.

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.410
And on October 28, 2020,

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:51.520
Kelly Boockvar, Secretary
of the Commonwealth,

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:52.780
issued a memorandum to that effect,

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.780
requiring all counties to sequester

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:57.870
those late arriving ballots,
which would then be counted

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.500
after all the other votes were processed.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:02.700
Now, just because they were
counted, didn't mean necessarily

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:05.630
that they would eventually be
added to the total vote count

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.750
in Pennsylvania, that
was still up in the air.

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:09.610
But three days after the
election, on November 6th,

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:11.310
the Trump campaign moved to intervene

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:12.670
as a plaintiff in the case.

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:14.073
The Trump campaign
asked the Supreme Court,

00:18:14.073 --> 00:18:16.890
seeking an emergency
order that all the ballots

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:19.170
be sequestered and not counted.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.350
The Trump campaign alleged
that it did not know

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.430
whether all counties were
following the procedures

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:24.900
set forth in Boockvar's memo.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.680
Now, most emergency applications
of public significance

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:29.680
are addressed by the full Supreme Court,

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:31.210
although particularly urgent matters

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.270
are sometimes acted on
by a single Justice,

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.800
while the other Justices
are later asked to weigh in.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:38.630
Here, Justice Alito
chose to do exactly that,

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:40.130
saying that he was referring the issue

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:41.930
to his colleagues for further action.

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:44.000
And in the meantime, he issued an order

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:46.230
requiring that the ballots be sequestered,

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:49.010
which again, at least if all the counties

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:50.480
were following the order

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.950
of the Secretary of the
Commonwealth, they already were.

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:55.320
But Alito also rejected the request

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.220
that the ballots not be counted.

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.190
And on November 7th, 2020,

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:01.800
Pennsylvania responded to the
Trump campaign's allegations

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:04.120
that some counties were not
appropriately sequestering

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:06.487
the ballots, saying quote,
"There is no evidence

00:19:06.487 --> 00:19:08.537
"that any County is
disobeying the clear guidance

00:19:08.537 --> 00:19:10.897
"to segregate these votes,
and the Republican Party

00:19:10.897 --> 00:19:13.627
"offers only speculation that
certain unidentified counties

00:19:13.627 --> 00:19:16.037
"may ignore the repeated guidance

00:19:16.037 --> 00:19:19.560
"or that the Secretary will
inconsistently change course."

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:21.310
So if the US Supreme Court

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:23.780
reversed the Pennsylvania
Supreme Court's ruling,

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:25.710
it would, in fact, invalidate ballots

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:27.540
received after Election day.

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.610
The exact number of
these ballots is unknown,

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:31.370
however, the impact of this decision

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:33.580
will almost certainly not be decisive.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:35.200
Pennsylvania counties
have been sequestering

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.470
the late arriving ballots
and will count them last,

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:39.040
and there probably aren't enough ballots

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:41.390
which arrived up to three
days after the election

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:42.810
to make up the vote differential,

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:44.880
if they even favored the President,

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.540
which is an open question.

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:48.510
So that takes us to the Pennsylvania cases

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.690
challenging the ballot cure process.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.400
There are still more cases
pending in Pennsylvania.

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:55.190
These cases challenge the state's process

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.950
for allowing voters to cure their ballots.

00:19:57.950 --> 00:19:59.370
The cure process was mandated

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:02.830
by the Help America Vote
Act of 2002 or HAVA.

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.820
The federal law requires
states to allow voters

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:07.210
to cure potential errors on their ballots,

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:08.330
and here's how that works.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.830
When there's an uncertainty
about a voter's eligibility,

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:12.210
such as an incorrect address,

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.320
the absence of required
identification or other issues,

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.650
the election official is
required to offer the voter

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:19.810
a provisional ballot
instead of a regular ballot.

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:21.830
If a voter votes in person,

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.670
they can ask for a provisional ballot,

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:25.780
or they may be directed by a poll worker

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:27.430
to vote by provisional ballot.

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:30.450
Since so many voters used a
mailed in ballot this year,

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.110
several states have created
a process for these voters

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:35.350
to fix certain errors in mailed ballots,

00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:37.850
like a missing or
mismatched voter signature.

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:39.600
Once a provisional ballot is cast,

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:41.220
it's stored separately
from the other ballots

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:43.540
and investigated by
local election officials.

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:46.010
Generally, a poll worker
will have to verify

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:48.040
the voter's identity
and eligibility to vote

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:50.970
and may require the voter to
provide further information.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:53.090
The states give the voters
the opportunity to cure

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:55.910
those defective ballots, and
each state has its own deadline

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:58.120
for voters who need to cure their ballots.

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:00.150
Here, Republicans are raising criticisms

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:03.100
of the state's processes
for curing ballots.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:05.730
So the Trump campaign and the
Republican National Committee

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:07.870
have now sued Boockvar,
who we just talked about,

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:09.820
claiming that she improperly
extended the deadline

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:13.530
for absentee and mail-in votes
to provide any missing proof

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:16.600
of identification from
November 9th to November 12th.

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:18.550
This is a challenge to
the state's cure process

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:19.790
for absentee ballots.

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:21.980
The Republicans contend that
it's wrong for the state

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:24.740
to give voters a few extra
days to cure deficiencies.

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.630
A judge ordered Boockvar to
segregate mailed-in ballots

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:29.700
from voters providing identification

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.330
between November 10th and November 12th,

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.500
saying that she would
rule later on the validity

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.370
of this deadline extension.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.810
And it's unlikely that
the cured ballot issue

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:40.510
will determine the result in Pennsylvania,

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:43.270
since these votes are not
appearing in the vote totals yet,

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:45.170
and most voters would
not have even attempted

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:46.510
to cure these ballots.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.275
But again, we're talking
about a pretty small number

00:21:48.275 --> 00:21:50.160
of ballots here, and it's going to be

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:52.368
a relatively easy decision
to see whether the court

00:21:52.368 --> 00:21:55.647
approves of the deadline extension or not.

00:21:55.647 --> 00:21:57.940
Additionally, Republican
candidates and officials

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:00.560
also sued election officials
in Montgomery County,

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:03.330
a heavily Democratic leaning
suburb of Philadelphia,

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:04.700
from notifying voters

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:07.350
and helping them to cure defective votes.

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:09.500
Plaintiffs withdrew their
request for an emergency order

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:12.650
segregating such votes just
days after filing the complaint,

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:14.760
and the judge said that
the case lacked merit

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.030
and failed to state a viable claim.

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:18.880
The bottom line in this
case is that the voters

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:20.690
have the right to cure their ballots,

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:22.930
provided that they follow
state requirements,

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:24.790
and the plaintiffs
couldn't come up with a way

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:26.610
that the cure process was violating law

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.950
in Montgomery County,
which takes us finally

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:32.140
to the poll observer
lawsuits in Pennsylvania,

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:33.070
which are not dissimilar

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:35.850
from some of the other poll
observer lawsuits that we saw,

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:37.750
though some of the outcomes of these cases

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:41.150
show what happens when you
piss off a federal judge.

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:43.630
In the first case, the Trump
campaign continued to file

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:45.350
cases claiming that
there were deficiencies

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.340
in how poll observers were treated.

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:49.760
The campaign successfully sued
Philadelphia on Election day,

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:51.817
asserting that its poll watchers
weren't being given, quote,

00:22:51.817 --> 00:22:54.497
"Reasonable access to
monitor the counting of votes

00:22:54.497 --> 00:22:57.537
"in Philadelphia, because they
were kept at too far distance

00:22:57.537 --> 00:22:58.860
"from the process."

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:01.340
The suit was dismissed by
a Philadelphia trial court,

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:03.920
but an appellate court
reversed, ordering officials

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:07.120
to allow all poll observers
to watch the ballot counting,

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.130
which frankly seems like
a win for all of us.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:12.150
We need both partisan and
neutral observers to make sure

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:13.980
that the election process
is working as it should

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:16.380
and there's no foul play, so
that we can all have confidence

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:18.460
that the democratic process was working.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:20.430
The only thing unusual
is that they were seeking

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:22.870
to invalidate the ballots,
but as far as I can tell,

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:24.570
no court has ever thrown out ballots

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:27.080
because poll watchers
were sitting too far away.

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.840
Which finally takes us to
the last election lawsuit

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:31.810
in Pennsylvania, where the Trump campaign

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:34.200
filed an emergency request
in the federal court

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:36.170
for the Eastern district of Pennsylvania

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:38.440
to stop the Philadelphia
County board of electors

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.797
from, quote, "Continuing
to count any ballots

00:23:40.797 --> 00:23:43.017
"so long as Republican
observers are not present

00:23:43.017 --> 00:23:44.740
"as required by state law."

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:47.560
And here, the plaintiffs ran
into an irascible federal judge

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:51.340
who simply wasn't having
allegations with no factual basis.

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:52.997
Here, Federal Judge Diamond asks,

00:23:52.997 --> 00:23:55.120
"Are your observers in the counting room?"

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.547
To which the Trump campaign
replied in an oral argument,

00:23:57.547 --> 00:24:00.840
"There is a non-zero number
of people in the room."

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:03.437
To which Judge Diamond responded,

00:24:03.437 --> 00:24:06.317
"I'm asking you as a member
of the bar of this court,

00:24:06.317 --> 00:24:09.167
"are people representing the
Donald J. Trump for president,

00:24:09.167 --> 00:24:11.980
"representing the
plaintiffs, in that room?"

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:14.300
To which the Trump campaign
lawyer responded, "Yes."

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:17.560
And Diamond said, "I'm sorry,
then what's your problem?"

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:18.670
So here's the thing.

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:20.917
When a federal judge asks you to respond

00:24:20.917 --> 00:24:22.940
"as a member of this court",

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:24.480
what he or she is signaling to you

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.600
is that you had better not lie

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:29.520
because the judge is
pissed off, and if you lie,

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:32.530
it is a violation of your lawyerly ethics

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:35.520
to respond to any tribunal
in a honest manner,

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:37.520
and you might be referred to bar counsel

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:40.080
and the judge will basically
take it as a personal offense.

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:43.580
So if you ever get that
question in a federal court,

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:45.790
you're not in a very good position.

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:47.870
Now, although the Trump
campaign continues to assert

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:50.160
in public that the counting
process is happening in secret

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:51.890
without any Republicans present,

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:54.447
when they're in court
in front of the judge,

00:24:54.447 --> 00:24:56.220
that's not what they're saying

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:59.140
and it appears because
that's just not true.

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:00.360
The parties in the case agreed

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:01.770
after Judge Diamond suggested it

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:04.220
that each party gets 60
observers in all areas

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:06.010
where ballots were being counted.

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:07.870
The judge agreed that
observers were allowed

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:11.180
but not at a certain distance,
only at an equal amount away.

00:25:11.180 --> 00:25:13.540
So this means that there
are 120 people watching

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:15.360
all the votes being
counted in Philadelphia,

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:18.200
and the city also has livestreamed
the entire vote count.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:19.430
You can actually find this on YouTube

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:21.340
and enjoy watching poll
workers putting ballots

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:22.980
in the machine all night long.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:25.230
So while the case settled
and might be considered a win

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:27.440
for the Trump campaign,
really it's a win for everyone

00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:29.710
to make sure that there
are an acceptable number

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:31.740
of observers watching the whole process.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:34.200
It's hard not to get behind that.

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:36.220
And finally, there was a brand new lawsuit

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:38.840
in Pennsylvania as of November 9th.

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:41.960
It basically claims that mail-in
voting is unconstitutional.

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:45.310
It claims that mail-in ballots
were not signature verified

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:47.280
to the same extent as the in-person votes,

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:48.720
creating a two tiered system

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.350
that's a constitutional violation.

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.130
It also rehashes the same allegations

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:54.150
of late received ballots
and lack of observers,

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:55.330
blah blah blah.

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:57.201
It's likely to fail pretty hard

00:25:57.201 --> 00:25:59.120
but they are swinging for the fences,

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:00.130
you have to give them credit for that.

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:02.440
They are asking for the
court to prevent Pennsylvania

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:06.210
from certifying results, so
that is terrifyingly hilarious

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.860
or hilariously terrifying,
you choose which.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:09.693
But at the end of the day,

00:26:09.693 --> 00:26:12.400
none of the cases involve
challenges to enough states

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.929
in any of the states
to change the results,

00:26:14.929 --> 00:26:18.050
let alone change the results
in several of the states,

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:19.420
and at the time of this recording,

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:23.170
it looks like Joe Biden's going
to win 306 electoral votes.

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:25.030
In the year 2000, it was very different.

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:27.920
The election came down to
a recount in a single state

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:29.850
where there were only 500 votes

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:31.130
separating the two candidates,

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:33.969
so a single court case
could decide the outcome

00:26:33.969 --> 00:26:37.380
of a huge state with
lots of electoral votes.

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:39.680
Here, there are just too many states

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:40.890
that would need to flip.

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:42.190
And of course there are reports

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:44.770
that the President is very
unhappy with his legal team

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:46.860
that seems to be run by Jared Kushner.

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:49.330
Man, is there anything
that that kid can't do?

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:52.670
And also the head of Citizens
United, David Bossie,

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:54.170
who actually isn't a lawyer.

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:56.670
I can't imagine why the
President is not happy

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:58.200
with his legal representation.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:26:59.592
And there doesn't seem to be any real,

00:26:59.592 --> 00:27:01.710
coordinated legal strategy here.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.200
There's nothing here that's
going to turn the tide

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:07.220
and there's no common theme
throughout these lawsuits

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:09.530
that's going to change the outcome

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:11.440
of the entire 2020 election.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:13.777
As Maggie Haberman of the
New York Times put it,

00:27:13.777 --> 00:27:16.867
"This is a media campaign
masquerading as a legal campaign

00:27:16.867 --> 00:27:18.130
"until proven otherwise."

00:27:18.130 --> 00:27:19.920
That seems to be about right.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.120
And not for nothing, the
Trump campaign's claims

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:24.210
outside of the court are very different

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:25.970
from the claims they are making in court,

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:27.950
because often you have
to make these claims

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:30.011
under the penalty of perjury,
and the lawyers themselves

00:27:30.011 --> 00:27:32.290
have certain ethical bounds,

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:34.570
and also there are potential sanctions

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:36.190
for filing frivolous lawsuits

00:27:36.190 --> 00:27:37.700
and putting things before the Court

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:40.760
that have a lack of complete basis.

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:42.930
And none of these lawsuits
are likely to change

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:43.930
the outcome of the election.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:45.880
The Trump campaign would
have to file and win lawsuits

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:48.420
concerning far more ballots
than he's litigating now.

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.360
And none of these lawsuits
would be able to overturn

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:52.527
the disparity in the various states,

00:27:52.527 --> 00:27:54.270
and the same goes for recounts.

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:56.010
Now, some states require the objector

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.770
to pay for the recount itself,

00:27:57.770 --> 00:27:59.440
so we'll see if the Trump campaign

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:01.350
actually ponies up the money to do that,

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:03.250
but no federal recount has
ever changed the results

00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:05.630
by more than 1/10th of a percent,

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:08.340
and the Trump campaign
would need to win lawsuits

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.750
in multiple states and
swing 18 electoral votes,

00:28:11.750 --> 00:28:13.780
a minimum of three states,
and that just doesn't seem

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:15.810
like it's in the realm of possibility.

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:18.300
But if you see election
fraud, or more likely,

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:20.601
an idiot wearing a "Beer,
Barbecue, Freedom" shirt,

00:28:20.601 --> 00:28:23.010
trying to interfere with
the poor election officials

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:25.810
